<DocsLayout title="Managed minor accounts">
  <h1>Managed minor accounts</h1>
  <p>
    A children's product needs more than an age band and a consent record: it
    needs to know <em>who a child belongs to</em>. Identity models that as a
    <strong>guardian edge</strong> between two accounts, and builds three things
    on it — a parent-creates-child signup, a guardian-authorized management
    surface over the child's account, and consent artifacts that satisfy COPPA,
    the UK Children's Code, and India's DPDP Rules 2025.
  </p>
  <p>
    The model is deliberate: <strong>children never self-sign-up</strong>. A
    parent creates their own adult account, then creates and runs each child's
    account from it.
  </p>

  <h2>Guardian edges</h2>
  <p>
    A guardian edge is a directed <code>guardian_user_id → child_user_id</code>
    fact, stored in its own project-scoped table. A child may have more
    than one guardian and an adult may guard many children;
    <code>(guardian, child)</code> is unique, so re-adding an edge is
    idempotent. Edges are project-scoped and carry the same row-level
    isolation as every other data-plane row.
  </p>
  <p>
    The edge is <em>live authorization state</em> and is deliberately distinct
    from the <code>ConsentRecord</code>, which is an audit artifact: the record
    survives deletion of either account, while the edge carries
    <code>users</code> foreign keys with <code>ON DELETE CASCADE</code> and dies
    with the accounts it references.
  </p>
  <p>Edges are created in three places:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Migration backfill</strong> — one edge per active consent whose
      adult and child both still exist, so consent already granted needs no
      re-collection.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>GrantParentalConsent</code></strong> — writes the edge in
      the same sequence as the consent record, before the child is activated.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong><code>CreateManagedChildAccount</code></strong> — writes the edge
      atomically with the account and the record.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    <code>guardian_user_id</code> always comes from the authenticated session,
    never from the request body. Two RPCs read the edge set:
    <code>ListManagedChildren</code> (self-only — the request carries no user id
    field at all) and <code>GetGuardians</code> (a guardian of that child, or a
    project admin).
  </p>

  <h2>Creating a child account</h2>
  <p>
    <code>CreateManagedChildAccount</code> turns one authenticated call from an
    adult into a working child account. The account is
    <strong>born <code>USER_STATUS_ACTIVE</code></strong> under the caller's
    guardianship — it never passes through
    <code>USER_STATUS_PENDING_PARENTAL_CONSENT</code>, because that state exists
    for a child who signed up with no consenting adult present, and here one is
    present and authenticated.
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`curl -X POST https://identity.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/CreateManagedChildAccount \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PARENT_ACCESS_TOKEN" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "username": "kid.one",
    "display_name": "Kid One",
    "date_of_birth_ms": 1420070400000,
    "password": "the-parent-chosen-password",
    "policy_version": "children-privacy-notice-v1",
    "step_up_password": "the-parents-own-password"
  }'`}
  />
  <p>
    The child is identified by a <strong>parent-chosen username</strong>
    (lowercase letters, digits, <code>_</code>, <code>-</code>, <code>.</code>;
    3–32 characters; unique within the project) because children below the
    ceiling frequently have no email address. That username is the login
    identifier: <code>PasswordLogin</code> accepts it in the <code>email</code>
    field whenever the value contains no <code>@</code>.
  </p>
  <p>
    Instead of <code>password</code> a caller may set
    <code>passkey_enrolment: true</code>. The response then carries an
    <code>enrolment_token</code> — a 15-minute ticket the child's own device
    presents to <code>BeginPasskeyRegistration</code> and
    <code>CompletePasskeyRegistration</code> to enrol a passkey without a
    session. The ticket authenticates nothing else; presenting both a session
    and a ticket is <code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code>.
  </p>

  <h3>What the call requires</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A strong verified factor</strong> on the adult's account —
      verified phone, a registered passkey, or an approved identity
      verification.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A step-up re-authentication</strong>: <code>step_up_password</code>
      is checked against the adult's stored hash on the call itself.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>A minor date of birth.</strong> A DOB that classifies as ADULT
      under the thresholds the child's market resolves to is rejected — this is
      not a route to creating adult accounts on someone else's behalf.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The account, the guardian edge, and the <code>ConsentRecord</code> (carrying
    <code>policy_version</code>, the verification factors, and
    <code>stepped_up</code>) commit in a single transaction: no partial state is
    reachable. The evidence is indistinguishable from a
    <code>GrantParentalConsent</code> grant, which is the point — one format for
    regulators, two ways of arriving at it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This is <strong>not self-signup</strong>, so the project's access mode
    (<code>open</code>, <code>allowlist</code>, <code>invite</code>,
    <code>closed</code>) does not gate it. The guard is the calling adult's own
    standing in the project, so parents can create children under
    <code>invite</code> and <code>closed</code> too.
  </p>

  <h2>Managing the account</h2>
  <p>
    Seven RPCs cover what a parent actually does. None of them needs the child's
    credentials, and none needs an admin credential.
  </p>
  <div class="table-scroll" tabindex="0" role="region" aria-label="Guardian management RPCs">
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr><th scope="col">RPC</th><th scope="col">Effect</th></tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>GetManagedChildProfile</code></td>
        <td>Reads the stored child record — deliberately little, since minor data-minimization suppresses optional PII on the paths that collect it</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>SetManagedChildPassword</code></td>
        <td>Sets the password directly (no email round-trip) and cuts the child's sessions</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>SetManagedChildUsername</code></td>
        <td>Renames the handle, with the format and uniqueness rules creation enforces</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>RevokeManagedChildSessions</code></td>
        <td>Invalidates every session and refresh token immediately — the lost-tablet case</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>DeactivateManagedChildAccount</code></td>
        <td>Suspends the account and cuts access; reversible by the same guardian</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>ReactivateManagedChildAccount</code></td>
        <td>Returns a deactivated account to active; refuses to move one out of <code>PENDING_PARENTAL_CONSENT</code></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>DeleteManagedChildAccount</code></td>
        <td>Runs the same hard-delete erasure cascade as the admin <code>DeleteUser</code> RPC</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Every one of them passes through <strong>one guard</strong>, so an operation
    added later is gated by construction. It requires both an active guardian
    edge from the session user to the child <em>and</em> a
    <code>step_up_password</code> re-entry on the call — a stolen session token
    must not be enough to take over a child's account.
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="bash"
    code={`curl -X POST https://identity.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/SetManagedChildPassword \\
  -H "Authorization: Bearer $PARENT_ACCESS_TOKEN" \\
  -H "Content-Type: application/json" \\
  -d '{
    "child_user_id": "usr_...",
    "new_password": "the-new-child-password",
    "step_up_password": "the-parents-own-password"
  }'`}
  />
  <p>
    A caller holding no edge receives an account-agnostic
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>, identical whether or not the child account
    exists, so the surface cannot be used to enumerate children.
  </p>

  <h3>Rights end when they should</h3>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Revoking consent ends management at once.</strong>
      <code>RevokeParentalConsent</code> deletes the edge, and the next call
      from that adult is refused. No grace period, no cached authorization.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Ageing out ends management too.</strong> A guardian edge to a user
      who has passed the adult threshold that applies to them stops conferring
      rights (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>) — an adult's account is their
      own. The band is re-derived on every call, so it takes effect on the
      birthday. The now-adult account's own live sessions are deliberately left
      running; ageing out is not a security event for the account holder.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>With age-gating off nothing changes.</strong> No band can be
      derived, so the edge alone authorizes — the behaviour a pre-gate
      deployment already had.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Deletion is immediate.</strong> It is the admin erasure cascade,
      not the 30-day self-service grace window. The <code>ConsentRecord</code>
      and the audit trail survive, per the retention posture consent records
      already carry.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Audit trail</h3>
  <p>
    Each operation emits its own event naming the acting guardian —
    <code>guardian_child_profile_viewed</code>,
    <code>guardian_child_password_set</code>,
    <code>guardian_child_username_changed</code>,
    <code>guardian_child_sessions_revoked</code>,
    <code>guardian_child_deactivated</code>,
    <code>guardian_child_reactivated</code>,
    <code>guardian_child_deleted</code> — on success <strong>and</strong> on
    refusal. A refusal carries <code>success=false</code> and a
    <code>step</code> detail naming the check that failed, so probing a child
    account is as visible in the trail as managing one. Account creation is
    audited as <code>managed_child_account_created</code>, and edges as
    <code>guardian_edge_created</code> / <code>guardian_edge_removed</code>.
  </p>

  <h2>Age thresholds are per jurisdiction</h2>
  <p>
    The child ceiling is not a constant. A project's <code>config_json</code>
    can carry a <code>jurisdictions</code> block, and an account's
    <code>market</code> selects which threshold pair classifies it — resolved in
    order: the account's stored market, the project's default jurisdiction, the
    strictest configured ceiling, then the deployment-wide
    <code>GATEWAY_AGEGATE_*</code> pair.
  </p>
  <Code
    lang="json"
    code={`{
  "jurisdictions": {
    "default": "IN",
    "thresholds": {
      "IN": { "child_max_age": 17, "adult_age": 18 },
      "US": { "child_max_age": 12, "adult_age": 18 },
      "GB": { "child_max_age": 12, "adult_age": 18 }
    }
  }
}`}
  />
  <p>
    An unresolvable market falls back to the strictest configured ceiling — it
    never drifts an account toward a more permissive regime. A granted
    <code>ConsentRecord</code> snapshots the market it resolved under, so the
    artifact says which jurisdiction's thresholds it proves consent against.
  </p>
</DocsLayout>
